Very encouraging.

75
00:05:33,460 --> 00:05:37,460
And today on the podcast, we're going to do a couple of things.

76
00:05:37,460 --> 00:05:43,719
We're going to talk a little bit about taking direction from youth leadership

77
00:05:43,719 --> 00:05:54,400
And I'm going to outline a little exercise you can do with your troop as a way of kind of identifying what's going on and where you hope to be in the future.

78
00:05:54,400 --> 00:06:03,020
Then we're going to talk a little bit about a post I had on the blog this week that was an answer to a question about Asperger's Syndrome.

79
00:06:03,359 --> 00:06:05,099
A little bit about special needs scouting.

80
00:06:05,100 --> 00:06:07,680
So that's a pretty full slate,

81
00:06:08,480 --> 00:06:16,760
Oh you know. but before we go on, let me tell you that this week I recorded an interview with Canadian author and canoe enthusiast, Kevin Callen.

82
00:06:17,620 --> 00:06:22,900
If you're familiar with Kevin and the guidebooks that he writes for Ontario, you're going to love it

83
00:06:22,980 --> 00:06:26,840
And we're going to make that part of a canoe show coming up soon.

84
00:06:27,160 --> 00:06:29,180
So I just wanted to let you know

85
00:06:29,400 --> 00:06:30,520
It was a fun interview.

86
00:06:30,960 --> 00:06:31,960
Kevin's a great guy.

87
00:06:32,120 --> 00:06:33,300
Hey, we need to keep moving.

88
00:06:33,480 --> 00:06:35,020
So let's get started, shall we?

89
00:06:35,100 --> 00:06:37,500
Scout Mastership, in seven minutes

90
00:06:38,400 --> 00:06:39,340
Or less

91
00:06:46,980 --> 00:06:52,700
We'll want to talk to you for a couple minutes about taking some direction from your youth leadership.

92
00:06:52,700 --> 00:06:57,240
And the goals of youth leaders and adults can kind of diverge sometimes

93
00:06:58,060 --> 00:07:02,200
And maybe we end up duplicating efforts or working at odds with each other.

94
00:07:02,580 --> 00:07:04,780
So we've got to kind of align those forces.

95
00:07:05,320 --> 00:07:06,360
We've got to have a plan,

96
00:07:06,700 --> 00:07:08,400
We've got to know what we're up to.

97
00:07:08,400 --> 00:07:19,840
Maintaining the integrity of youth leadership means that they have the opportunity to plan and act independent of adult micromanagement and adult coercion,

98
00:07:19,840 --> 00:07:29,900
And this exercise I'm going to describe will help define the roles of adult and youth leaders while balancing the oversight and involvement of adults.

99
00:07:29,900 --> 00:07:33,659
So this is a very easy process.

100
00:07:34,080 --> 00:07:40,919
First of all, I ask everybody in a leadership position to write down three personal scouting goals for themselves

101
00:07:40,920 --> 00:07:43,960
And three goals they have for the troop.

102
00:07:45,400 --> 00:07:47,440
Now, this isn't always easy,

103
00:07:48,080 --> 00:07:51,020
But writing the goals down is a crucial step in the process.

104
00:07:51,220 --> 00:07:57,160
Writing requires some more thought and commitment to the idea than just sitting there and talking about them.

105
00:07:57,160 --> 00:08:07,840
In a group setting, scouts will a lot of times latch on to somebody else's idea to the exclusion of their own, or they won't really think of something.

106
00:08:08,420 --> 00:08:16,100
So making the whole goal setting thing a group discussion doesn't work as well as saying,

107
00:08:16,100 --> 00:08:20,800
Okay here, just write down three things that you want to get out of scouting,

108
00:08:21,420 --> 00:08:26,760
And then write down three ways that you want to move the troop forward.

109
00:08:27,160 --> 00:08:34,760
And then what happens is in the second step, adult leaders need to meet on their own and discuss their goals

110
00:08:35,600 --> 00:08:42,240
And they identify shared personal goals and work on the next immediate steps to achieve them.

111
00:08:42,680 --> 00:08:50,140
And then we discuss the troop goals and identify those aspects of them that should be worked on by the adult leadership

112
00:08:50,140 --> 00:08:53,840
and which we should suggest to the scouts for action.

113
00:08:53,840 --> 00:09:01,480
Then, instead of having the scouts meet initially to discuss their goals,

114
00:09:02,320 --> 00:09:07,300
we just will interview each youth leader individually and discuss them

115
00:09:07,660 --> 00:09:13,500
And we'll help them identify the immediate next step to achieve their personal goals,

116
00:09:13,500 --> 00:09:16,560
And then we'll discuss their goals for the troop.

117
00:09:17,080 --> 00:09:21,960
What they see or hear or experience in their leadership role is very revealing

118
00:09:22,420 --> 00:09:28,000
And it's important to interview them individually because they may have a perspective on things

119
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:31,860
that they wouldn't express as fully when they're in a group of their peers.

120
00:09:32,500 --> 00:09:38,820
This process over time has revealed several things that might never have come to light otherwise.

121
00:09:38,820 --> 00:09:43,340
You know, scouts are at an age where they're very sensitive to the censorship of their peers.

122
00:09:44,400 --> 00:09:49,540
These individual interviews are just casual discussions with myself and my assistant scoutmasters.

123
00:09:49,800 --> 00:09:56,840
If the scout in question has a father serving as an assistant scoutmaster in another adult leadership role,

124
00:09:57,360 --> 00:09:59,280
he's not part of the interview.

125
00:09:59,280 --> 00:10:09,839
Then, once we've done this, we move on and we identify the shared goals that represent maybe new ideas or directions for the troop,

126
00:10:10,319 --> 00:10:13,699
And we develop a prioritized list of those goals.

127
00:10:14,600 --> 00:10:20,400
So by the time you get to this point, you're going to have a list of goals for the troop

128
00:10:20,400 --> 00:10:26,680
And they're going to be shared between several members of the adult and the youth leadership, no doubt,

129
00:10:27,140 --> 00:10:30,720
And then you can begin discussing them and figuring out. okay, so what happens next?

130
00:10:31,260 --> 00:10:32,400
How do we make this happen?

131
00:10:33,240 --> 00:10:37,880
At the end of the process, both youth and adults are going to be on the same track

132
00:10:38,520 --> 00:10:43,480
And they're going to have a common set of goals and the associated tasks to achieve them.

133
00:10:43,480 --> 00:10:50,300
As an added benefit, differences of opinion can be resolved and rivalries can be quashed

134
00:10:50,620 --> 00:11:01,780
And those who are not really doing much or don't feel a part of the whole gang and the leadership part of the troop will be brought on in.

135
00:11:02,640 --> 00:11:03,980
So let's go over that again.

136
00:11:04,520 --> 00:11:05,660
It's very, very simple:

137
00:11:05,660 --> 00:11:10,120
Write down three goals for yourself personally in scouting-

138
00:11:10,400 --> 00:11:13,900
And this is for all the youth leaders and all the adult leaders-

139
00:11:14,360 --> 00:11:18,620
And then write three goals down that are more of a general nature for the troop,

140
00:11:19,760 --> 00:11:23,640
And then the adults will meet and discuss what they have written first,

141
00:11:23,640 --> 00:11:37,620
And then the adults will sit down and interview each one of the youth leaders individually so that, instead of being under the pressure of a group of scouts,

142
00:11:37,900 --> 00:11:43,180
they'll be able to just talk about exactly what they're thinking and what they want to do,

143
00:11:43,600 --> 00:11:53,420
And then that gets boiled down to a list for a wider group discussion and the identification of next actions to start working towards those goals.

144
00:11:53,640 --> 00:11:55,180
It can really help.

145
00:11:55,460 --> 00:11:57,600
We try and put this into practice every year

146
00:11:57,819 --> 00:12:06,600
Or if we feel, like you know, things have gotten a little diluted and we're unfocused in our efforts. this is a good exercise to do,

147
00:12:07,080 --> 00:12:11,340
So I hope you find it useful and I hope it helps you build your troop

148
00:12:11,800 --> 00:12:15,900
Green, grow the rushes. oh, what is your 12-0??

149
00:12:15,939 --> 00:12:17,720
12 for the 12 apostles,

150
00:12:17,980 --> 00:12:21,680
11 for the 11 that went to heaven and 10 for the 10 commandments,

151
00:12:21,680 --> 00:12:25,680
9 for the 9 bright shiners and 8 for the April rainers,

152
00:12:25,900 --> 00:12:27,660
7 for the 7 stars in the sky,

153
00:12:27,839 --> 00:12:29,459
6 for the 6 brown walkers,

154
00:12:29,699 --> 00:12:31,380
5 for the cymbals at your door

155
00:12:31,479 --> 00:12:33,140
And 4 for the gospel makers.

156
00:12:33,439 --> 00:12:38,180
3,, 3, the rivals,

157
00:12:40,579 --> 00:12:42,760
2,, 2, the lily white boys,

158
00:12:42,900 --> 00:12:44,880
Carved all in green. oh,

159
00:12:44,880 --> 00:12:50,439
1 is one and all alone and evermore shall be so.

160
00:12:50,440 --> 00:13:19,480
Here's a question for Scouting Magazine's next edition of the column. What Would You Do?

161
00:13:19,480 --> 00:13:24,140
used to be frontline stuff. if you go back that far.

162
00:13:24,480 --> 00:13:25,360
Here's what it says.

163
00:13:25,480 --> 00:13:29,820
One of our boys has Asperger's Syndrome.

164
00:13:30,760 --> 00:13:36,820
He does well learning Scouting concepts by reading, but he has struggled with campouts and activities that are not highly structured.

165
00:13:37,820 --> 00:13:40,060
What can we do as a troop to help him succeed?

166
00:13:40,060 --> 00:13:44,359
Also, it's taking him a long time to complete advancement requirements.

167
00:13:44,920 --> 00:13:50,739
How do we encourage him to keep putting in the effort to complete his requirements, even though they are more challenging for him than other scouts?

168
00:13:51,199 --> 00:13:52,839
Here's what I answered.

169
00:13:53,459 --> 00:14:02,060
People with Asperger's Syndrome have difficulty recognizing nonverbal communications and the clues of social interaction, like facial expressions.

170
00:14:02,060 --> 00:14:10,699
So imagine that everyone around you is wearing a mask and you can't read their facial expressions and you have some idea of what it might be like.

171
00:14:11,640 --> 00:14:20,640
I would encourage your scouts and leaders to ask and answer questions to the scout in a very nonjudgmental way. watching the tone of your voice and the way that you ask questions,

172
00:14:20,640 --> 00:14:26,040
That will help him read the social clues that he might not otherwise recognize.

173
00:14:26,760 --> 00:14:32,319
As for requirements, you know, I'd just ask him if he's having any particular difficulties and offer to help.

174
00:14:33,100 --> 00:14:39,460
Remember that scouts get to advance at their own speed and level of interest and I would probably encourage him as I would any other scout.

175
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:45,380
I've had the opportunity to work with scouts with Asperger's and it challenged me to look at the world a bit differently

176
00:14:45,380 --> 00:14:50,800
And I think I ended up learning a lot more from them than I ever was able to teach them.

177
00:14:52,480 --> 00:15:03,160
I put this on the blog last week and it got some interesting comments. when that blog entry hit Facebook,

178
00:15:04,080 --> 00:15:10,500
Christine said I have a fifth grader with mild Asperger's who is crossing over to the Boy Scouts tomorrow night.

179
00:15:10,500 --> 00:15:18,680
I really appreciate all the people like you who take the time to understand him and others like him and to help them succeed.

180
00:15:21,060 --> 00:15:26,780
Jerry Scoutmaster, Jerry from the SMM podcast said this:

181
00:15:27,160 --> 00:15:30,040
I have three scouts in my troop that have autism.

182
00:15:30,380 --> 00:15:32,760
Two have been diagnosed with Asperger's.

183
00:15:33,380 --> 00:15:37,220
They love structure and do not do well with sudden changes.

184
00:15:37,220 --> 00:15:43,040
I think the best thing that any scout leader can do is to not treat them a whole lot differently than any other scout.

185
00:15:43,400 --> 00:15:48,500
They need to be in a patrol, they need to go on campouts and they need to work on advancement, just like everybody else.

186
00:15:48,960 --> 00:15:57,240
Scoot Looney, Scoot Looney- I will assume that that is a nom de plume, but he said:

187
00:15:57,320 --> 00:16:00,240
In the last five years I've been working with multiple scouts with autism.

188
00:16:00,600 --> 00:16:04,800
I've learned a great deal, and so should any leader that has been blessed with these young men.

189
00:16:04,800 --> 00:16:09,979
I'm the father of an autistic scout who is a star rank and an assistant scoutmaster,

190
00:16:10,520 --> 00:16:19,099
Part of my wood badge ticket. I created a website to let the parents of autistic kids know that the scouting program could be of great benefit to them

191
00:16:19,099 --> 00:16:23,540
and to help scoutmasters that have autistic scouts in their unit,

192
00:16:23,979 --> 00:16:25,260
And there's going to be a link to that.

193
00:16:25,260 --> 00:16:37,900
It's a really good resource, but there'll be a link to that site on the podcast blog entry.

194
00:16:38,819 --> 00:16:40,960
And then Walter had this to say:

195
00:16:41,040 --> 00:16:44,920
The first thing I would do, now that I found it would be to read the BSA publication

196
00:16:45,080 --> 00:16:46,920
Scanning for Youth with Disabilities,

197
00:16:46,920 --> 00:16:51,719
specifically the sections on autism and spectrum disorder.

198
00:16:52,219 --> 00:16:59,839
I will also have a link to that document on the blog in the post that contains this podcast.

199
00:17:01,240 --> 00:17:07,319
This is much, much improved from the material available. 10 years ago, when my son was going through cubs with disabilities,

200
00:17:08,520 --> 00:17:09,720
Scouter Adams said:

201
00:17:09,720 --> 00:17:11,579
My son has Asperger's.

202
00:17:12,420 --> 00:17:15,940
Pat currently has another scout on the spectrum as well.

203
00:17:16,640 --> 00:17:19,560
Every person who has this is a different case, so we work with it.

204
00:17:19,920 --> 00:17:22,400
People with Asperger's can be very high-functioning.

205
00:17:23,020 --> 00:17:27,420
For me working with my son and others who have it, I have to just relate to them slightly differently.

206
00:17:27,760 --> 00:17:30,860
Overall, work with the boys adults who have it.

207
00:17:31,240 --> 00:17:32,480
You can really learn something.

208
00:17:33,120 --> 00:17:34,420
And then Sean said:

209
00:17:34,420 --> 00:17:37,560
My son has Asperger's

210
00:17:37,860 --> 00:17:41,940
And we have to do things a little more structured and sometimes more coaching and teaching is in order.

211
00:17:42,600 --> 00:17:45,220
Autistic scouts are really no different than any other scout.

212
00:17:45,360 --> 00:17:52,740
Each situation, whether it's a quote, normal unquote scout or an Asperger's scout, will present itself in a different manner.

213
00:17:54,360 --> 00:17:58,540
It's up to the scoutmasters and assistant scoutmasters to be able to work with this scout

214
00:17:58,540 --> 00:18:01,660
and make decisions that any good leader has to make.

215
00:18:01,660 --> 00:18:04,980
I've had boys come through the troop in the past couple of decades

216
00:18:04,980 --> 00:18:10,880
who looked at the world a little differently and didn't relate in the same way socially

217
00:18:11,700 --> 00:18:16,960
And, you know, they were just kind of a little bit of outside the circle.

218
00:18:17,520 --> 00:18:26,120
Then, as time has gone by, we've gotten a lot better at identifying this thing that's called the autism spectrum

219
00:18:26,120 --> 00:18:32,959
And if you study a little bit about it, I think one of the things you walk away with is: we're all on the autism spectrum somewhere,

220
00:18:33,840 --> 00:18:34,040
You know.

221
00:18:34,540 --> 00:18:41,520
I mean there are people who function very highly and then there are people who have a little bit more difficulty and challenge.

222
00:18:42,179 --> 00:18:45,379
But really I think we're all on there

223
00:18:45,760 --> 00:18:48,860
And we've kind of begun to redefine normal.

224
00:18:48,860 --> 00:18:58,939
We've certainly redefined the way that we approach people with cognitive differences and with different ways of social interaction,

225
00:18:59,740 --> 00:19:04,560
And we've done that because we understand that this is not a choice that they're making.

226
00:19:04,939 --> 00:19:08,020
This is just simply the way that their brain is built.

227
00:19:08,020 --> 00:19:20,379
When I think of this subject, I think of one boy who was in the troop several years ago who had been identified as having Asperger's and was really, really difficult.

228
00:19:21,900 --> 00:19:26,780
He was just socially, had real, real problems.

229
00:19:27,600 --> 00:19:30,460
He couldn't deal with what was going on around him.

230
00:19:30,600 --> 00:19:31,040
He couldn't.

231
00:19:31,200 --> 00:19:33,620
He found it very difficult to relate to other scouts.

232
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:35,360
He would occasionally get violent.

233
00:19:35,360 --> 00:19:43,659
His way of self-regulating would be to walk away from whatever activity he was involved in if he began to get on overload.

234
00:19:44,360 --> 00:19:48,959
And that could be very disturbing because all of a sudden we would look around and we would see that he wasn't there.

235
00:19:50,520 --> 00:19:53,959
And this was when he was, you know, 11,, 12,, 13..

236
00:19:54,459 --> 00:19:56,199
And we had some really trying times

237
00:19:56,820 --> 00:20:04,219
And it got so trying that at one point his parents decided that, you know, he needed to step away from scouting for a while,

238
00:20:04,220 --> 00:20:09,420
While they really worked at figuring out exactly how to do this,

239
00:20:10,040 --> 00:20:14,140
And they really worked at working with his counselors and doctors,

240
00:20:14,560 --> 00:20:17,520
And they did this over the course of several weeks one summer

241
00:20:17,760 --> 00:20:23,600
And he returned to us with the right medications and things like that,

242
00:20:23,600 --> 00:20:25,419
And he was able to focus better

243
00:20:25,540 --> 00:20:27,659
And he was able to socially interact better

244
00:20:28,280 --> 00:20:32,260
And he did just a wonderful job of being a scout

245
00:20:32,919 --> 00:20:40,560
And he, you know, we still worked with him in the same way that we would work with all of our other scouts,

246
00:20:41,120 --> 00:20:47,840
Just understanding that it was like working with a person who maybe spoke a bit of a different language,

247
00:20:47,840 --> 00:20:49,899
And so we learned to speak his language

248
00:20:50,300 --> 00:20:51,840
And it wasn't really all that difficult

249
00:20:52,620 --> 00:20:57,179
And, you know, learning to speak his language kind of broadened my world a bit

250
00:20:57,399 --> 00:21:05,139
And being able to look at and understand the way that he was processing things and the way that he was understanding and interacting with the world,

251
00:21:05,500 --> 00:21:07,020
It was a real eye-opener

252
00:21:07,020 --> 00:21:14,720
And I owe him a debt of gratitude for having been able to stick it out with us and to work through that.

253
00:21:15,139 --> 00:21:23,980
And he's gone on, you know, graduated from high school, went to college to a very prestigious university over in England

254
00:21:24,660 --> 00:21:26,580
And continues to study there,

255
00:21:26,900 --> 00:21:32,040
Has gotten an undergraduate degree or the equivalent thereof over there and is working on a graduate degree now

256
00:21:32,660 --> 00:21:35,000
And is a very impressive young man.

257
00:21:35,000 --> 00:21:40,540
So these things are sometimes terrifying for parents

258
00:21:41,340 --> 00:21:44,540
And it's sometimes a bit terrifying for volunteer leaders too,

259
00:21:44,720 --> 00:21:48,440
Because we receive relatively little training in this type of thing.

260
00:21:49,120 --> 00:22:00,580
As the comments that you saw or that I read out indicate, there's a lot of kids who are identified either on the autism spectrum somewhere

261
00:22:00,580 --> 00:22:04,780
or are identified specifically with this Asperger's syndrome,

262
00:22:05,740 --> 00:22:12,960
And it's well worth taking the time to understand it and to begin to learn how to work with them.

263
00:22:12,960 --> 00:22:24,460
It's all too easy, you know, when you're dealing with a large group of boys, to marginalize some and to separate them out

264
00:22:24,460 --> 00:22:34,300
because you lack the time or the will or the empathy to try and understand what they're doing and understand how they're looking at the world.

265
00:22:34,300 --> 00:22:38,540
So this is a subject that bears much broader conversation

266
00:22:39,340 --> 00:22:43,960
And I hope to do that soon on the blog and the podcast

267
00:22:44,180 --> 00:22:51,780
And to put together, you know, some good resources for people who are working with Scouts, with Challenges of all kinds.

268
00:22:51,780 --> 00:23:02,820
But the two references that we had here- the scouting document about working with Scouts and youth with disabilities

269
00:23:02,820 --> 00:23:08,540
and the website about autism and Boy Scouts- I will make sure to post those on the blog.

270
00:23:08,720 --> 00:23:15,300
Just some thoughts to get you going and some encouragement that, if you are finding yourselves working with boys

271
00:23:15,300 --> 00:23:22,180
who are identified with these types of challenges, it can be a little daunting and it can be a little terrifying,

272
00:23:23,200 --> 00:23:29,700
But the story is only being half told in what you're seeing when they're 11 or 12 or 13 or 14..

273
00:23:30,620 --> 00:23:33,040
Their story goes on and on and on

274
00:23:33,440 --> 00:23:37,260
And I can tell you that it can be a wonderful, very positive story

275
00:23:37,260 --> 00:23:45,580
And it's something that you will look back on and see as a very important part of your life as well as theirs.

